@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+# Claude Code review customization
+
+This fork's `custom` branch adds a Japanese review guide, a fullscreen WezTerm launcher, and read-only GitHub review context. Vim-style navigation is preserved.
+
+## Setup
+
+Requires macOS with WezTerm, Git, and Claude Code. GitHub context also requires authenticated `gh`. Build with Rust (`cargo build --release --locked`), then copy `target/release/lumen` to `~/.local/bin/lumen-custom`. The normal Homebrew `lumen` installation can coexist.
+
+From this checkout:
+
+```sh
+cargo build --release --locked
+mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin"
+cp target/release/lumen "$HOME/.local/bin/lumen-custom"
+claude plugin marketplace add "$PWD" --scope user
+claude plugin install lumen-review@lumen-local --scope user
+```
+
+Reload plugins in your existing Claude Code session with `/reload-plugins`, then run `/lumen-review:review`. The session's working repository opens in a separate fullscreen WezTerm window. Submitted feedback returns through the original tool call to the same session. The launcher uses a temporary WezTerm configuration; it does not change your terminal configuration.
+
+To try it outside Claude Code:
+
+```sh
+~/.local/bin/lumen-custom review --repo "$PWD" --github-reviews
+```
+
+## 操作
+
+画面下部に日本語の操作ガイドを常時表示します。`?` で詳しいヘルプを開けます。
+
+- `j/k`：移動、`1`：ファイル一覧、`2`：差分、`Cmd+B`：一覧の表示 / 非表示
+- ドラッグ：範囲選択、`c`：コメント、`Enter`：保存、`Ctrl+J`：改行
+- `Shift+C`：自分のコメント一覧
+- `F2`：GitHubレビューを表示。`n/p` で次・前、`j/k` でスクロール、`c` で自分のコメント
+- `s`：確認後、コメントをClaude Codeへ送信
+- `q`：送信せず終了（GitHub画面では差分へ戻る）
+
+GitHubレビューは起動時に現在のブランチのPRから取得します。行コメント、レビュー本文・承認状態、PR会話を閲覧できます。自分のコメントを付けたレビューは、投稿者・URL・元の本文とともに送信します。GitHubへの返信・投稿は行いません。PRがない場合や取得に失敗した場合もローカル差分をレビューできます。現時点では github.com が対象です。
+
+全画面とは専用WezTermウィンドウの全画面表示です。Claude Codeの非対話シェル内にTUIを埋め込む方式ではありません。レビュー中のコメントはメモリ上に保持され、送信せず閉じると破棄されます。
+
+## Development checks
+
+```sh
+cargo test --locked --bin lumen
+```
+
+The review parser/export and guide layout have unit tests. The local implementation was also exercised in a pseudo-terminal: create a diff comment, open imported GitHub context, add a local note, confirm submission, and check the exported text. Cancellation was checked separately. GitHub API responses in that interaction test were fixtures; no GitHub comments were posted.
+
+## Claude Code sandbox
+
+The launcher needs macOS GUI/XPC access and a Unix socket. The skill requests `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` for this one Bash invocation, through Claude Code's normal permission flow. Approve the launch if prompted. It does not change global sandbox settings; if your policy rejects this request, the skill stops. A sandboxed launch may fail with `Operation not permitted` or an XPC connection error.
+
+WezTerm can include credentials from environment variables in startup errors. The launcher therefore returns a fixed diagnostic instead of raw child logs.
+
+## WezTerm shortcut (Cmd+Shift+R)
+
+Install `scripts/review-shortcut.sh` as `~/.local/bin/lumen-review-shortcut`. Configure WezTerm's Cmd+Shift+R callback to run `{ wezterm.home_dir .. "/.local/bin/lumen-review-shortcut", cwd, tostring(pane:pane_id()) }` with `SpawnCommandInNewTab`, where `cwd` is the source pane's repository. It runs `lumen-custom diff` directly in a new tab of the existing WezTerm window, preserving the user’s font and color settings. The local Cmd+Shift+R callback makes that window fullscreen if needed. It does not start a separate terminal process or invoke a Claude Bash tool.
+
+On `s` and confirmation, feedback is pasted into the source pane as a draft. Press Enter in Claude Code to submit it. Use the shortcut while that pane is at Claude Code's input prompt. Cancellation does not paste anything. If delivery fails, a private temporary file preserves the feedback and its location is shown.
+
+## Review key customization
+
+- `1` / `2`: focus files / diff; arrows navigate the focused area.
+- `Cmd+B`: toggle the file sidebar. The local WezTerm configuration forwards this as F3 to the TUI.
+- `Tab`: toggle unified and side-by-side views. Unified shows deleted lines (red, `-`) followed by added lines (green, `+`) in each changed block. Comments retain the original LEFT/RIGHT source line numbers. Up/Down visits every row; Shift+Up/Down extends within the selected source side.
+- `c`: add a comment, including in the GitHub panel; `Shift+C`: list local diff comments.
+- `?`, then `h`: toggle the persistent shortcut guide. The preference is saved in `~/.config/lumen/review-guide`. The help title shows ON/OFF. Arrows and Vim navigation remain available in the detailed help.
+
+## Keyboard line comments and selected guide items
+
+Select a file, then press `v` to highlight a line in the diff. Use Up/Down to move to the desired line. Hold Shift with Up/Down to extend or shrink the range. Left/Right switches to the old/new side in the two-column view. Press `c` to comment on the highlighted line(s), then Enter to save. Esc clears the selection. Keyboard movement skips rows without a line on the selected side; added/deleted files select the available side.
+
+In `?` help, press `p` to choose which shortcuts remain visible. Up/Down selects an item and Space toggles its checkbox; Esc returns to help. Choices are saved in `~/.config/lumen/review-shortcuts.json`. The existing `h` toggle still hides or shows the entire guide without discarding the choices.
+
+To exercise the keyboard workflow in an isolated Git repository and home directory (Unix):
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/review_keyboard.py target/release/lumen
+```
+
+The test does not type into a live WezTerm or Claude Code session.
+
+## File browsing
+
+Review starts with focus in the file sidebar. Up/Down (or j/k) changes the highlighted file and previews its diff immediately, without Enter. Enter moves focus into the preview; `1` returns to the sidebar. Directory rows retain Enter to expand/collapse.
+
+Entering the diff with Enter or `2` now starts keyboard line selection automatically. Switching files while in the diff also selects a line in the new file. The initial sidebar preview retains file-navigation focus; `v` is still available to start or restart line selection explicitly.
